    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In the GeneScale project, the proposed CRISPR-Cas9 system is employed as a precision molecular scalpel to excise deleterious mutations within the CFTR gene locus, targeted at lung epithelial cell type, significantly implicated in aberrant mucus secretion. The endeavor consists of a multistep process of LNP-mediated targeted delivery (for bioformulation: cationic lipid selection for membrane penetration, cholesterol and phospholipid bilayer optimization for LNP stability and cellular uptake, and PEG-coated surface modification to enhance circulation time and biocompatibility), succeeding in a pulmonary administration pipeline mediated via a nebulizer. Encapsulated Cas9-gRNA ribonucleoprotein complexes demonstrate preferential parceling into the lung parenchyma, effectuating a genome editing campaign aimed at abolishing the pathogenic transmembrane conductance regulator (CFTR) protein variants. Quantitative spectrometry (like qPCR) and plasmid sequencing are leveraged to discern mutational correction and gene expression normalization by top-tier molecular diagnostic tools. Ensuring accurate gene editing and mitigating inadvertent genomic disarray, whole-genome sequencing (WGS) is utilized to pinpoint off-target mutagenesis (utilizing alignment-based bioinformatics analyses against reference human genomes) and",
      "source": "target",
